# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CONTENT [CONTENT]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# 3-Block Data Structure: Configuration → Operations → Validation
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CONFIGURATION [Cc]
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# # Cc.1 Message Syntax [SYNTAX]
#
#   Purpose:  Configuration — how to write commit messages
#   Contains: Format rules and conventions
#
# Format Rules:
#   - Lines starting with # are comments (removed from final message)
#   - Blank lines are preserved
#   - Subject line: 72 chars max (brief, imperative)
#   - Body lines: wrap at 72 chars
#   - Leave blank line between subject and body
#   - Body uses category-based organization with bullet points
#   - Attribution block required at end
#
# Subject Format:
#   [repo-name] TYPE: Brief description — sub-description
#
# Types (Building Block Method):
#   Foundation (0):  INIT, SEED, ANCHOR
#   Build Up (+1):   BUILD, EXPAND, CONNECT
#   Break Down (-1): REFINE, DISTILL, FIX
#   Operations:      SYNC, MIGRATE, CLEAN
#
# Repo Names (lowercase):
#   bereshit, tal, omnicode, millennium-os, etc.

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPERATIONS [Co]
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# IMPORTANT: Content below is the actual template that git uses.
# Documentation comments above are stripped; content below appears in editor.
#
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# # Co.1 Commit Message [MESSAGE]
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SUBJECT LINE (72 chars max)
# Format: [repo-name] TYPE: Brief description — sub-description
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[repo-name] TYPE: Brief description — sub-description

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BODY (blank line above required, wrap at 72 chars)
# Category-based organization with bullet points
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Category Name:
- Specific change with context
- Another change in this category

Another Category:
- What was done and why it matters
- Additional details

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REFERENCES (optional — inline or at end)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# refs: CWS-STD-### or document key
# part: Milestone or phase name

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ATTRIBUTION (required)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Generated by CreativeWorkzStudio LLC

Co-Authored-By: Nova Dawn
Co-Authored-By: Seanje Lenox-Wise (Lead) <seanje.lenox@creativeworkzstudio.com>

# # Co.2 Reference Guide [REFERENCE]
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REPO NAME EXAMPLES (lowercase)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# [bereshit]      - Foundation repository (anchor of anchors)
# [tal]           - Tenebris and Lux game
# [omnicode]      - OmniCode language compiler
# [millennium-os] - MillenniumOS operating system
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TYPE REFERENCE (Building Block Method)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Foundation (0) — Establishing anchors:
#   INIT    - Initialize new component/structure
#   SEED    - Plant crystallized understanding
#   ANCHOR  - Establish fixed reference point
#
# Build Up (+1) — Constructing from foundation:
#   BUILD   - Create/construct new capability
#   EXPAND  - Extend existing functionality
#   CONNECT - Link components together
#
# Break Down (-1) — Refining toward essence:
#   REFINE  - Improve/polish existing work
#   DISTILL - Extract essential understanding
#   FIX     - Correct issues/errors
#
# Operations — Maintenance:
#   SYNC    - Synchronize between locations
#   MIGRATE - Move/restructure content
#   CLEAN   - Remove obsolete/cleanup
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VALIDATION [Cv]
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# # Cv.1 Validation Rules [RULES]
#
# The commit-msg hook validates:
#   - Repo name is lowercase alphanumeric with hyphens: [repo-name]
#   - Type is valid Building Block Method type
#   - Description starts with uppercase
#   - Description doesn't end with period
#
# Bypass (emergency only): git commit --no-verify

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# END CONTENT
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CLOSING [CLOSING]
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# 3-Block Data Structure: Operational guidance and closing notes
#
# Section order: Policy → Extension → Troubleshooting → Reference → Note → Template → Omission
# Flow: modify → extend → debug → lookup → ground → instantiate → reserve
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# # X.1 Policy [POLICY]
#
#   Purpose:  Guide future maintainers — what's safe to change
#   Contains: Safe / Careful / Never modification levels
#
# Safe to Modify:
#   ✅ Add project-specific domain examples (Co.2)
#   ✅ Add project-specific commit types (when approved)
#   ✅ Add team-specific references (issue tracker, etc.)
#   ✅ Adjust wording in body section prompts
#
# Modify with Care:
#   ⚠️ Removing body sections (may lose valuable context)
#   ⚠️ Changing keying format (affects commit history consistency)
#   ⚠️ Adding required fields (may slow down developers)
#
# NEVER Modify:
#   ❌ Remove the keying format ([DOMAIN/path] TYPE: Description)
#   ❌ 3-block structure (METADATA, CONTENT, CLOSING)
#   ❌ Building Block Method type categorization
#
# Security: Commit messages are public — never include secrets, API keys,
# sensitive paths, or credentials in messages.

# # X.2 Extension [EXTENSION]
#
#   Purpose:  How to expand this template — mirrors M.10 Roadmap
#   Contains: Extension patterns, integration points
#
# Adding New Commit Types:
#   1. Add to commit-msg.go ValidTypes slice
#   2. Add to Co.2 Reference Guide in appropriate category
#   3. Rebuild and deploy commit-msg hook
#
# Adding New Domains:
#   1. Add to commit-msg.go ValidDomains slice
#   2. Add example to Co.2 Domain Examples
#   3. Rebuild and deploy commit-msg hook
#
# Adding Body Sections:
#   1. Add to Co.1 Message template
#   2. Update Co.2 reference if needed
#   3. Consider validation in commit-msg hook
#
# Architecture: See CONTENT for Cc/Co/Cv structure.
# Roadmap: See M.10 for planned enhancements.

# # X.3 Troubleshooting [TROUBLESHOOTING]
#
#   Purpose:  Common problems and solutions — gitmessage issues
#
# "Template not appearing":
#   → Check: git config commit.template
#   → Ensure path is absolute or relative to repo root
#
# "Wrong editor opens":
#   → Set editor: git config --global core.editor "code --wait"
#   → Or: git config --global core.editor "vim"
#
# "Commit rejected by hook":
#   → Check domain: CWS, CPSI, LANG, OS, IDE, GAME, TMPL
#   → Check type against Building Block Method types
#   → Ensure description starts with uppercase, no trailing period
#
# "Hook not running":
#   → Check: git config core.hooksPath
#   → Verify hook is executable: chmod +x commit-msg
#
# Pattern-specific troubleshooting in Cv.1 Validation Rules.

# # X.4 Reference [REFERENCE]
#
#   Purpose:  Quick lookup — copy-paste ready commands
#
# Configuration:
#   git config commit.template .gitmessage     # Local (per-repo)
#   git config --global commit.template ~/.gitmessage  # Global
#
# Verification:
#   git config commit.template                 # Check current template
#   git config core.hooksPath                  # Check hooks path
#   git commit                                 # Test (opens editor)
#
# Hook bypass (emergency only):
#   git commit --no-verify -m "emergency: ..."
#
# Debug:
#   cat .gitmessage | grep -v '^#'             # Show uncommented lines

# # X.5 Closing Note [NOTE]
#
#   Purpose:  Final guidance and grounding — echoes M.8
#   Verse:    Proverbs 25:11
#
# Commit messages are the story of your codebase. Each commit is a chapter
# that future developers (including your future self) will read to understand
# why decisions were made. Write with grace and clarity.
#
# "A word fitly spoken is like apples of gold in pictures of silver."
#   — Proverbs 25:11
#
# May your commits tell a story worth reading.

# # X.6 Template Guidance [TEMPLATE]
#
#   Purpose:  How to use this template — remove when instantiating
